# Kanban board app — local settings.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the repo root (the directory you start the app
# from) and edit the values.  `.env` is gitignored: it holds machine-local
# settings and is never committed.
#
# Both settings are resolved as: process environment first, then this file.
# An exported variable wins; the file is the fallback for launches that cannot
# export variables (a double-clicked launcher, a scheduled task, a service).

# --- Agent1 sync master switch -------------------------------------------------
# The Work button and every outbound card op are gated on TWO independent
# switches, and BOTH must be on:
#
#   1. this process-level master switch, KANBAN_SYNC_ENABLED;
#   2. the per-board "Agent1 sync" checkbox in the Boards dialog, stored in the
#      board file as {"sync": {"agent1": true}}.
#
# The checkbox is NOT the master switch — it only satisfies gate 2.  With this
# variable unset (and no .env) the app fails closed and Work answers:
#   "agent1 sync is not enabled for this board"
# which names the checkbox that is correctly ticked.  Set this to 1 to open
# gate 1.  Off values: 0, false, no, off (case-insensitive).  Anything else
# counts as on.
KANBAN_SYNC_ENABLED=1

# --- Shared queue root ---------------------------------------------------------
# Where board files and the two sync queues live:
#   <KANBAN_WORKING_DIR>/queue-kanban-to-agent1/messages.jsonl  (we write)
#   <KANBAN_WORKING_DIR>/queue-agent1-to-kanban/messages.jsonl  (we read)
#
# Agent1 resolves the SAME variable in its own repo.  Both sides must agree on
# one physical directory, or messages pile up in two disjoint trees and nothing
# syncs.  Default: "data" relative to the app's working directory.
# Set it to an absolute path when the app is launched from elsewhere — that is
# exactly the case where a relative default silently points at an empty tree.
KANBAN_WORKING_DIR=C:\Dev\Kanban\data
